<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-5-1-BasicNotions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Basic Notions</title>

  <p>
    The notion of function is one of the fundamental concepts of mathematics.
    Functions appear in calculus classes in the form of relations between two
    variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such as
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>y=ax+b</m> (linear functions),
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <m>y=x^{2}</m> (quadratic functions),
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <m>y=\sin x</m> (trigonometric functions),
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <m>y=2^{x}</m> (exponential functions) and <m>y=\log_{2}x</m>
          (logarithm functions),
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    and so on.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-func">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be two non-empty sets. A function
        <m>f: A\rightarrow B</m> from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> is a correspondence
        that associates to each element <m>a\in A</m> a unique element
        <m>f(a)</m> of <m>B</m>. The set <m>A</m> is called the domain of
        <m>f</m> and the set <m>B</m> is called the codomain of <m>f</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    To avoid unnecessary repetition, when dealing with a function
    <m>f:A\rightarrow B</m>, the underlying assumption will be that <m>A</m> and
    <m>B</m> are non-empty sets.
  </p>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In calculus, the domain and the codomain of functions were often not
        explicitly stated and were understood to be both the set of real number
        <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <p>
    It is worth emphasizing that a function <m>f:A\rightarrow B</m> consists of
    three things:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          the domain <m>A</m>,
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          the codomain <m>B</m>,
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          the rule/formula that associates to each element <m>a\in A</m> a
          unique element <m>f(a)\in B</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f:A\rightarrow B</m> and <m>g:C\rightarrow D</m> be two
        functions. We say that the functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are equal or
        the same if
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>A=C</m>,
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>B=D</m>,
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              for any <m>x\in A=C, f(x)=g(x)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="exa-function1to5_2n">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Define <m>f:\{1,2,3,4,5\}\rightarrow \mathbb{N}</m>, where
        <md>
          f(1)=2, f(2)=4, f(3)=6, f(4)=8, f(5)=10,
        </md>
        and
        <m>g:\{n: n\in \mathbb{N}, 1\leq n\leq 5\}\rightarrow \mathbb{N}</m>,
        where
        <md>
          g(k)=2k, \forall k\in \{n:n\in \mathbb{N}, 1\leq n\leq 5\}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are equal because
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              their domains are the same <m>\{1,2,3,4,5\}</m>,
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              their codomains are identical <m>\mathbb{N}</m>, and
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f(k)=g(k)</m> for any <m>k\in \{1,2,3,4,5\}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Define the following functions
        <md alignment="align">
          <mrow>f:\mathbb{N}\rightarrow \mathbb{N}, f(n)&amp;=2n+1, \forall n\in \mathbb{N},</mrow>
          <mrow>g:\mathbb{N}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}, g(n)&amp;=2n+1, \forall n\in \mathbb{N},</mrow>
          <mrow>h:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}, h(n)&amp;=2n+1, \forall n\in \mathbb{R}.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Any two of these functions are different:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f\neq g</m> because the codomains are different (the codomain
              of <m>f</m> is <m>\mathbb{N}</m> which is different from
              <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, the codomain of <m>g</m> ).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>g\neq h</m> because the domains are different (the domain of
              <m>g</m> is <m>\mathbb{N}</m> which is not the same as
              <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, the domain of <m>h</m> ).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>h\neq f</m> because either the domains are different or the
              codomains are not the same.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="exa-function_x2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the following functions:
        <md alignment="align">
          <mrow>f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}, f(x)&amp;=x^{2}, \forall x\in \mathbb{R},</mrow>
          <mrow>g:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow [0,+\infty), g(x)&amp;=x^{2}, \forall x\in \mathbb{R},</mrow>
          <mrow>h:[0,+\infty)\rightarrow [0,+\infty), h(x)&amp;=x^{2}, \forall x\in [0,+\infty).</mrow>
        </md>
        The functions <m>f,g</m>, and <m>h</m> are not the same because
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> have different codomains,
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> have different domains,
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>h</m> and <m>f</m> have different domains (and codomains).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <definition xml:id="def-graph_function">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f:A\rightarrow B</m> be a function from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>. The
        graph of the function <m>f</m> is the set of ordered pairs
        <md>
          Graph(f)=\{(a,f(a)):a\in A\}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

</section>